    focus: 'Maratha Empire: Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj (1627/1630–1680), born at Shivneri Fort, father Shahji Bhonsle, mother Jijabai, guardian Dadoji Kondadev, spiritual guru Samarth Ramdas; Guerrilla warfare / Ganimi Kava; Early conquests: Torna fort 1646, Raigad, Kondana/Sinhagad; Killed Afzal Khan of Bijapur with tiger claws (Bagh Nakh) 1659 at Pratapgad; Attack on Shaista Khan at Pune 1663; Sacked Surat port 1664 & 1670; Treaty of Purandar 1665 with Raja Jai Singh of Amber, surrendered 23 of 35 forts; Visited Aurangzeb court at Agra 1666, imprisoned in Jaipur Bhavan, escaped in sweet baskets; Coronation at Raigad on 6 June 1674 by Vishweshwar / Gaga Bhatt of Varanasi, assumed title Chhatrapati and Haindava Dharmoddharak; Ashta Pradhan Council: 1. Peshwa (Prime Minister), 2. Amatya/Majumdar (Finance), 3. Wakia-Navis/Mantri (Interior/Intelligence), 4. Samant/Dabir (Foreign Affairs), 5. Sachiv/Shurnavis (Royal Correspondence), 6. Senapati/Sari Naubat (Commander-in-Chief), 7. Nyayadhish (Chief Justice), 8. Panditrao (Charity/Religion); Taxation: Chauth (1/4th or 25% of land revenue paid by neighboring states to prevent Maratha raids) and Sardeshmukhi (additional 10% levy as hereditary overlord of Maharashtra); Successors: Sambhaji (1680-1689), Rajaram, Tarabai, Shahu; Peshwa Era (1713-1818): Balaji Vishwanath (1st Peshwa 1713), Baji Rao I (1720-1740, Hindu Pad Padshahi, Strike at the trunk of the withering tree, Battle of Palkhed 1728), Balaji Baji Rao / Nana Saheb (1740-1761, 3rd Battle of Panipat 14 Jan 1761 where Ahmad Shah Abdali defeated Maratha army led by Sadashivrao Bhau and Vishwasrao); Vijayanagara Empire (1336–1646): Founded in 1336 on bank of Tungabhadra river by brothers Harihara I and Bukka I of Sangama dynasty, inspired by Saint Vidyaranya and Sayana; Capital Hampi (Vijayanagara); 4 Dynasties: 1. Sangama (1336-1485, Deva Raya I visited by Nicolo Conti 1420; Deva Raya II visited by Abdur Razzaq 1443), 2. Saluva (1485-1505, Saluva Narasimha), 3. Tuluva (1505-1570, founded by Vira Narasimha), 4. Aravidu (1570-1646, Tirumala); Krishnadeva Raya (1509–1529, greatest ruler of Tuluva dynasty, titles Andhra Bhoja, Abhinava Bhoja, Yavana-rajya-sthapaka; Duarte Barbosa and Domingo Paes Portuguese travelers visited; court poet patron of Ashtadiggajas in Telugu literature, headed by Allasani Peddana author of Manucharitam, Tenali Ramakrishna author of Panduranga Mahatmyam; authored Amuktamalyada in Telugu on polity and Jambavati Kalyanam in Sanskrit; built Hazara Rama temple and Vittala temple at Hampi with musical pillars, built Nagalapuram city); Battle of Talikota / Rakshasi-Tangadi / Bannihatti (23 Jan 1565): Combined forces of 4 Deccan Sultanates (Bijapur, Golconda, Ahmednagar, Bidar; Berar did not participate) defeated Vijayanagara army under Aliya Rama Raya, leading to destruction of Hampi; Administration: Nayankara system (military chiefs / Amara-nayakas given lands), Ayagar system in villages; Bhakti & Sufi Movements: Bhakti Movement originated in South India by Alvars (12 Vaishnavite saints, Nammalvar, Andal only female saint) and Nayanars (63 Saivite saints, Appar, Sambandar, Sundarar); Propagators: Adi Shankaracharya (8th century, Advaita / Non-dualism, established 4 Mathas: Sringeri South, Dwarka West, Puri East, Badrinath North), Ramanujacharya (11th century, Vishishtadvaita, Sri Vaishnavism), Madhvacharya (Dvaita), Nimbarkacharya (Dvaitadvaita), Vallabhacharya (Shuddhadvaita, Pushtimarga), Ramananda (brought Bhakti to North India, preached in Hindi, 12 disciples including Kabir, Ravidas, Sena, Dhanna, Pipa), Kabir Das (Nirguna saint, weaver of Varanasi, Dohas compiled in Bijak: Sakhi, Sabad, Ramaini), Guru Nanak Dev (1469–1539, born at Talwandi/Nankana Sahib, founded Sikhism, Langar/community kitchen, Guru Granth Sahib), Mirabai (Saguna Krishna devotee of Mewar, Bhajans in Braj/Rajasthani), Tulsidas (contemporary of Akbar, authored Ramcharitmanas in Awadhi, Vinaya Patrika, Kavitavali), Chaitanya Mahaprabhu (Gaudiya Vaishnavism, Sankirtan, Kirtana movement in Bengal), Shankaradeva (Ekasarana Dharma in Assam, Sattriya dance, Borgeet); Sufi Movement: 12 Silsilas (orders); Chishti Silsila (founded in India by Khwaja Moinuddin Chishti 1192 at Ajmer / Garib Nawaz; Bakhtiyar Kaki, Baba Farid Ganj-i-Shakar whose verses in Guru Granth Sahib, Nizamuddin Auliya of Delhi / Mahbub-i-Ilahi who saw 7 Sultans, Amir Khusrau disciple, Sheikh Salim Chishti of Fatehpur Sikri); Suhrawardi Silsila (Bahauddin Zakariya, Multan); Qadiri Silsila (Sheikh Abdul Qadir Jilani, Dara Shikoh follower); Naqshbandi Silsila (orthodox, Khwaja Baqi Billah, Sheikh Ahmad Sirhindi Mujaddid Alf-i-Sani, opposed Akbar Din-i-Ilahi).'
