    focus: 'Pre-Congress Political Organizations: Landholders Society 1838 (Dwarkanath Tagore), British India Society 1839, East India Association 1866 London (Dadabhai Naoroji), Poona Sarvajanik Sabha 1870 (M.G. Ranade, G.V. Joshi), Indian League 1875 (Shishir Kumar Ghosh), Indian Association 1876 (Surendranath Banerjee and Ananda Mohan Bose, held Indian National Conference 1883 & 1885), Madras Mahajan Sabha 1884 (M. Veeraraghavachariar, G. Subramania Iyer, P. Anandacharlu), Bombay Presidency Association 1885 (Pherozeshah Mehta, K.T. Telang, Badruddin Tyabji); Foundation of Indian National Congress (INC): Founded on 28 December 1885 by retired British civil servant Allan Octavian Hume (A.O. Hume, Father of INC); Viceroy at the time was Lord Dufferin; First session held at Gokuldas Tejpal Sanskrit College, Bombay (originally planned at Pune, moved due to cholera outbreak); First President: Womesh Chandra Bonnerjee (W.C. Bonnerjee); Attended by 72 delegates; Safety Valve Theory propounded by Lala Lajpat Rai (in Young India 1916) and William Wedderburn (biographer of Hume); Moderate Phase (1885–1905): Key Moderate leaders: Dadabhai Naoroji (Grand Old Man of India, Drain of Wealth theory, Poverty and Un-British Rule in India 1901, first Indian elected to British House of Commons 1892 from Finsbury on Liberal ticket, INC President 3 times: 1886 Calcutta, 1893 Lahore, 1906 Calcutta where term Swaraj was first used), Gopal Krishna Gokhale (Political guru of Mahatma Gandhi, Servants of India Society 1905 Pune, Benaras INC Session 1905), Surendranath Banerjee (Rashtraguru, A Nation in Making, skipped 1st INC session due to Indian National Conference), Pherozeshah Mehta (Lion of Bombay, Bombay Chronicle), Dinshaw Wacha, Badruddin Tyabji (1st Muslim INC President, Madras 1887), George Yule (1st British/European INC President, Allahabad 1888); Methods: 3Ps (Petitions, Prayers, Protests), constitutional agitation within legal framework; Extremist / Militant Phase (1905–1919): Lal-Bal-Pal trio: Lala Lajpat Rai (Punjab Kesari/Sher-e-Punjab, Arya Samajist, Young India, Unhappy India), Bal Gangadhar Tilak (Lokmanya, "Swaraj is my birthright and I shall have it / स्वराज मेरा जन्मसिद्ध अधिकार है और मैं इसे लेकर रहूँगा", Kesari in Marathi, Mahratta in English, organized Ganpati Festival 1893 and Shivaji Festival 1895, authored Gita Rahasya in Mandalay jail, Valentine Chirol called him "Father of Indian Unrest"), Bipin Chandra Pal (New India journal, Bengal orator), Aurobindo Ghosh (Vande Mataram newspaper, Doctrine of Passive Resistance, Karmayogin, Savitri, retired to Pondicherry ashram after Alipore Bomb Case 1908); Partition of Bengal (1905): Announced on 20 July 1905 by Viceroy Lord Curzon; Partition came into effect on 16 October 1905 (observed as National Day of Mourning, Raksha Bandhan day suggested by Rabindranath Tagore, singing of Amar Shonar Bangla); Bengal partitioned into: 1. Western Bengal, Bihar & Orissa (capital Calcutta, Hindu majority), 2. Eastern Bengal & Assam (capital Dacca, Muslim majority, Lt. Gov. Bampfylde Fuller); Swadeshi & Boycott Movement (1905–1908): Formal boycott resolution passed on 7 August 1905 at Calcutta Town Hall; Boycott of British Manchester cloth, Liverpool salt, foreign goods, government schools, titles; National Council of Education set up 15 August 1906, Bengal National College (Aurobindo Ghosh 1st Principal); Bengal Chemical Swadeshi Stores founded by Acharya Prafulla Chandra Ray; Swadesh Bandhab Samiti set up by Ashwini Kumar Datta in Barisal; Women and students participated in mass picketing; Spread outside Bengal: Tilak and daughter Ketkar in Poona & Bombay, Lala Lajpat Rai and Ajit Singh in Punjab, Syed Haider Raza in Delhi, Chidambaram Pillai in Madras (Swadeshi Steam Navigation Company at Tuticorin); Foundation of All-India Muslim League (30 December 1906 at Dacca): Founded by Nawab Salimullah of Dacca, Nawab Mohsin-ul-Mulk, and Waqar-ul-Mulk; 1st President: Aga Khan III; Headquarter at Lucknow; Surat Split of INC (December 1907, 23rd Session): Session held on bank of Tapti river at Surat (originally scheduled at Nagpur); President: Rash Behari Ghosh; Clash between Moderates and Extremists over presidency and Swadeshi resolutions; Extremists expelled from Congress; Morley-Minto Reforms / Indian Councils Act 1909: Introduced Separate Communal Electorates for Muslims (Lord Minto called "Father of Communal Electorate"); S.P. Sinha appointed first Indian member of Viceroy Executive Council; Annulment of Partition of Bengal (1911): Delhi Durbar held in December 1911 by Lord Hardinge II in honor of King George V and Queen Mary; Partition of Bengal annulled; Capital of India officially shifted from Calcutta to Delhi in 1911 (operational in 1912); Bihar and Orissa separated from Bengal.'
