    focus: 'Mahatma Gandhi Early Life & South Africa (1869–1914): Born 2 October 1869 at Porbandar Gujarat, father Karamchand (Kaba), mother Putlibai; Studied law in London (Inner Temple); Went to South Africa 1893 to represent Dada Abdullah; Evicted from first-class train carriage at Pietermaritzburg station June 1893; Founded Natal Indian Congress 1894; Indian Opinion journal; Established Phoenix Settlement 1904 Durban and Tolstoy Farm 1910 Johannesburg; First Satyagraha against Asiatic Registration Act 1906; Authored Hind Swaraj 1909 on ship; Returned to India on 9 January 1915 (Pravasi Bharatiya Divas); Founded Sabarmati Ashram 1917 Ahmedabad; Political guru Gopal Krishna Gokhale; Early Satyagrahas in India: 1. Champaran Satyagraha 1917 (Bihar): First Civil Disobedience in India; Rajkumar Shukla invited Gandhi to protest Tinkathia system (compulsory cultivation of Indigo on 3/20th of land); Accompanied by Rajendra Prasad, J.B. Kripalani, Mahadev Desai, Narhari Parikh; Tinkathia abolished, 25% refund secured; Rabindranath Tagore conferred title "Mahatma"; 2. Ahmedabad Mill Strike 1918 (Gujarat): First Hunger Strike in India; Dispute over Plague Bonus between mill owners (Ambalal Sarabhai) and workers; Gandhi fast led to 35% wage hike; Anusuya Sarabhai aided Gandhi; 3. Kheda Satyagraha 1918 (Gujarat): First Non-Cooperation movement; Crop failure in Kheda, British revenue code exempted tax if yield was under 1/4th, but authorities demanded full revenue; Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel emerged as key lieutenant; Government agreed to exempt poor peasants; Rowlatt Act & Jallianwala Bagh Massacre (1919): Anarchical and Revolutionary Crimes Act / Rowlatt Act passed March 1919 (No Dalil, No Vakil, No Appeal - indefinite detention without trial for 2 years); All-India Satyagraha Day 6 April 1919; Arrest of Dr. Saifuddin Kitchlew and Dr. Satyapal in Punjab; Jallianwala Bagh Massacre on 13 April 1919 (Baisakhi day) at Amritsar: Brigadier-General Reginald Dyer ordered open fire on peaceful gathering; Hundreds killed; Hunter Commission 1919 appointed to investigate (Disorders Inquiry Committee); Rabindranath Tagore renounced Knighthood; Gandhi surrendered Kaisar-i-Hind gold medal (awarded for Boer War ambulance service); Udham Singh assassinated Michael O Dwyer (Lt. Gov of Punjab) in London on 13 March 1940; Khilafat & Non-Cooperation Movement (1920–1922): Khilafat Movement led by Ali Brothers (Maulana Shaukat Ali and Mohammad Ali Jauhar), Maulana Abul Kalam Azad; Protested dismemberment of Ottoman Empire and stripping of Caliph (Khalifa) powers by Treaty of Sevres; All-India Khilafat Committee elected Gandhi as President in Nov 1919; Non-Cooperation Movement approved at Special Calcutta Session Sept 1920 (Pres: Lala Lajpat Rai) and ratified at Nagpur Annual Session Dec 1920 (Pres: C. Vijayaraghavachariar); Programs: Boycott of government colleges, courts, foreign goods, surrender of titles, promotion of Charkha, Khadi, national schools (Kashi Vidyapeeth, Jamia Millia Islamia, Gujarat Vidyapeeth founded); Tilak Swaraj Fund created 1921, raised 1 crore rupees; Chauri Chaura Incident (4 February 1922): Peasant procession attacked police station at Chauri Chaura near Gorakhpur (UP), killing 22 policemen; Deeply shocked by violence, Gandhi suspended Non-Cooperation Movement on 12 February 1922 at Bardoli Congress Working Committee meeting; Gandhi arrested on 10 March 1922, sentenced to 6 years by Judge C.N. Broomfield; Swaraj Party (1923): Formed on 1 January 1923 by Chittaranjan Das (President / Deshbandhu) and Motilal Nehru (Secretary) to enter legislative councils ("End or Mend the councils"); Pro-changers vs No-changers (Rajendra Prasad, C. Rajagopalachari, Vallabhbhai Patel); Vithalbhai Patel elected first Indian Speaker (President) of Central Legislative Assembly in 1925; Simon Commission & Nehru Report (1927–1928): Indian Statutory Commission appointed Nov 1927 headed by Sir John Simon; 7 members, all British (White Commission); Arrived in Bombay 3 Feb 1928, greeted with black flags and "Simon Go Back"; Lala Lajpat Rai martyred following lathi charge at Lahore; Nehru Report (August 1928): Drafted by Motilal Nehru committee in response to Lord Birkenhead challenge; Demanded Dominion Status, Fundamental Rights, rejected separate electorates; Jinnah rejected it and gave 14 Points (March 1929); Lahore Session & Purna Swaraj (December 1929): Presided by Jawaharlal Nehru; Passed historic "Purna Swaraj" (Complete Independence) resolution; Tricolour flag hoisted on bank of Ravi river at midnight of 31 December 1929; 26 January 1930 declared as first Independence Day of India; Civil Disobedience Movement & Dandi March (1930): Gandhi launched Dandi March from Sabarmati Ashram on 12 March 1930 with 78 chosen followers; Reached Dandi coast in Navsari Gujarat on 5 April 1930 (240 miles in 24 days); Broke Salt Law on 6 April 1930 by picking lump of salt; Salt Satyagraha across India: C. Rajagopalachari led Vedaranyam Salt March (Trichinopoly to Vedaranyam in Tamil Nadu); K. Kelappan led march from Calicut to Payyanur in Kerala; Sarojini Naidu, Manilal Gandhi, and Imam Saheb led raid on Dharsana Salt Works Gujarat (21 May 1930, Web Miller American journalist reported brutal police beatings); Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan (Frontier Gandhi / Badshah Khan) organized Khudai Khidmatgars (Red Shirts / Surkh Posh) in NWFP; Rani Gaidinliu led Naga revolt; Round Table Conferences (RTC, London 1930–1932): British PM Ramsay MacDonald; 1st RTC (Nov 1930-Jan 1931): Boycotted by Congress; Gandhi-Irwin Pact / Delhi Pact (5 March 1931): Brokered by Tej Bahadur Sapru and M.R. Jayakar; Civil Disobedience suspended, Congress agreed to attend 2nd RTC; Endorsed at Karachi Congress Session March 1931 (Pres: Vallabhbhai Patel, passed resolutions on Fundamental Rights and National Economic Programme drafted by Nehru); 2nd RTC (Sept-Dec 1931): Attended by Mahatma Gandhi as sole representative of Congress (travelled on SS Rajputana accompanied by Sarojini Naidu, Madan Mohan Malaviya, Mahadev Desai); Failed due to Jinnah and Ambedkar demands for separate electorates; Communal Award (16 August 1932): Announced by Ramsay MacDonald, granted separate electorates to Depressed Classes; Gandhi began fast-unto-death in Yerwada Jail Poona on 20 Sept 1932; Poona Pact (24 September 1932): Signed between Dr. B.R. Ambedkar and Madan Mohan Malaviya (on behalf of caste Hindus); Abandoned separate electorates for Depressed Classes; In return, reserved provincial assembly seats for Depressed Classes increased from 71 to 147, and 18% in Central Legislature; Gandhi founded All-India Anti-Untouchability League 1932 and Harijan weekly newspaper; 3rd RTC (Nov-Dec 1932): Congress did not attend; Dr. B.R. Ambedkar and Tej Bahadur Sapru attended all three Round Table Conferences.'
