    focus: 'Government of India Act 1935: Abolished Dyarchy in provinces and introduced Provincial Autonomy; Proposed All-India Federation and Dyarchy at Center (federal part never implemented); Established Federal Court (1 Oct 1937, Sir Maurice Gwyer 1st Chief Justice) and Reserve Bank of India (1 April 1935); 1937 Provincial Elections: Congress formed ministries in 8 out of 11 provinces (absolute majority in Madras, UP, Bihar, Central Provinces, Orissa; coalition in Bombay, Assam, NWFP); Resignation of Congress Ministries (October 1939): Protesting British dragging India into World War II without consulting Indian leaders; Muslim League observed "Deliverance Day / Day of Deliverance / मुक्ति दिवस" on 22 December 1939 (joined by Ambedkar); August Offer (8 August 1940): Proposed by Viceroy Lord Linlithgow; Offered Dominion Status after war and expansion of Viceroy Executive Council; Rejected by Congress and Muslim League; Individual Satyagraha (October 1940): Launched by Gandhi to assert freedom of speech against war; Known as "Delhi Chalo Satyagraha"; 1st Individual Satyagrahi: Acharya Vinoba Bhave (inaugurated at Pavnar ashram Maharashtra on 17 Oct 1940); 2nd Satyagrahi: Jawaharlal Nehru; 3rd Satyagrahi: Brahma Dutt; Cripps Mission (March 1942): Led by Sir Stafford Cripps to seek Indian support in WWII; Offered Dominion Status after war, Constituent Assembly, and right of provinces to secede from Union; Gandhi called it "A post-dated cheque on a crashing bank / दिवालिया बैंक के नाम उत्तर-तिथीय चेक"; Quit India Movement / August Kranti (August 1942): All-India Congress Committee (AICC) historic session at Gowalia Tank Maidan (now August Kranti Maidan), Bombay on 8 August 1942; Quit India Resolution drafted by Nehru, approved under presidency of Maulana Abul Kalam Azad; Gandhi gave historic mantra: "Do or Die / करो या मरो"; Operation Zero Hour (midnight of 8-9 August 1942): All top leaders arrested immediately; Gandhi, Sarojini Naidu, Kasturba Gandhi imprisoned at Aga Khan Palace Pune; Nehru, Azad, Patel, Kripalani imprisoned at Ahmednagar Fort; Mahadev Desai and Kasturba died in Aga Khan Palace; Movement became spontaneous leaderless revolution; Underground Leadership: Aruna Asaf Ali (Heroine of 1942, hoisted tricolour at Gowalia Tank on 9 Aug), Usha Mehta (operated secret underground Congress Radio from Bombay), Jayaprakash Narayan (escaped from Hazaribagh Central Jail on Diwali 1942, organized Azad Dasta in Nepal), Ram Manohar Lohia, Achyut Patwardhan, Sucheta Kripalani; Parallel Governments (Prati Sarkar): 1. Ballia (UP): First parallel government formed by Chittu Pandey (Sher-e-Ballia) in August 1942, 2. Tamluk / Jatiya Sarkar (Midnapore Bengal): Organized Vidyut Vahini, 73-year-old Matangini Hazra martyred holding national flag, 3. Satara (Maharashtra): Longest running parallel government led by Nana Patil and Y.B. Chavan; Indian National Army (INA / Azad Hind Fauj): Idea first conceived by Captain Mohan Singh in Malaya (1942) with Japanese Major Fujiwara; Rash Behari Bose founded Indian Independence League in Tokyo (March 1942); Subhas Chandra Bose (Netaji): Born 23 January 1897 at Cuttack Odisha, cleared ICS 1920 but resigned, political guru C.R. Das; Congress President at Haripura Session 1938 and Tripuri Session 1939 (defeated Gandhi candidate Pattabhi Sitaramayya, Gandhi: "Sitaramayyas defeat is my defeat"); Resigned from Congress and founded Forward Bloc at Unnao UP in May 1939; Escaped house arrest from Calcutta on 16 January 1941 (Great Escape) via Peshawar, Kabul, Moscow to Berlin; Met Hitler, established Free India Centre and Indian Legion in Germany, Jai Hind greeting coined; Reached Singapore by submarine U-180 and I-29 in July 1943; Assumed leadership of INA on 25 August 1943; Proclaimed Provisional Government of Free India (Arzi Hukumat-e-Azad Hind) at Singapore on 21 October 1943; Slogans: "Delhi Chalo", "Give me blood, and I will give you freedom / तुम मुझे खून दो, मैं तुम्हें आजादी दूंगा"; Formed INA Brigades: Subhas, Gandhi, Nehru, Azad, and all-women Rani of Jhansi Regiment (commanded by Captain Lakshmi Swaminathan/Sahgal); Japan handed over Andaman and Nicobar Islands to Netaji, renamed Shaheed Dweep and Swaraj Dweep (Dec 1943); Reportedly died in plane crash at Taihoku Taiwan on 18 August 1945; Red Fort Trials / INA Trials (November 1945): Joint trial of Colonel Prem Sahgal, Colonel Gurbaksh Singh Dhillon, and Major Shah Nawaz Khan at Red Fort Delhi; Defence committee headed by Bhulabhai Desai, joined by Tej Bahadur Sapru, Jawaharlal Nehru, Kailash Nath Katju, Asaf Ali; Sentenced to deportation, but Commander-in-Chief Claude Auchinleck remitted sentences due to massive nationwide public outcry; Royal Indian Navy (RIN) Mutiny (18-23 February 1946): Ratings of HMIS Talwar mutinied in Bombay protesting racial discrimination and poor food, led by B.C. Dutt; Spread to Karachi and 78 ships; Surrendered on 23 February 1946 after intervention of Sardar Patel and Jinnah; Cabinet Mission Plan (March 1946): Sent by British PM Clement Attlee; 3 British Cabinet Ministers: Lord Pethick-Lawrence (Secretary of State, Chairman), Sir Stafford Cripps (President Board of Trade), A.V. Alexander (First Lord of Admiralty); Arrived Delhi 24 March 1946; Rejected demand for full Pakistan; Proposed Union of India with 3-tier grouping of provinces (Section A: Hindu majority, Section B: Muslim majority NW, Section C: Muslim majority East); Proposed Constituent Assembly elected by provincial assemblies; Interim Government formed on 2 September 1946 headed by Jawaharlal Nehru (Vice-President of Viceroy Executive Council); Muslim League joined on 26 October 1946 (Liaquat Ali Khan Finance Minister); Mountbatten Plan / June 3rd Plan (1947): Lord Mountbatten arrived as last Viceroy in March 1947; Announced partition of India on 3 June 1947; Accepted by Congress and Muslim League; Indian Independence Act 1947: Passed by British Parliament on 18 July 1947; Partition of British India into two independent dominions: India and Pakistan; Boundary Commission headed by Sir Cyril Radcliffe (Radcliffe Line); Attainment of Independence on 15 August 1947; Nehru historic "Tryst with Destiny" midnight speech; Lord Mountbatten 1st Governor-General of independent India, C. Rajagopalachari first and only Indian Governor-General (1948–1950).'
